@Naseem77 thanks — all four reproduced and fixed in 25cc2f0. Each has a regression test that failed before the change.

**1. Default `finalize()` handed an ambiguous mention to a row (High)** — confirmed: 3 nodes with `resolve=False`, 2 with the default. The deduplicator's phase-1 refusal was not carried into phase 3, and `LLMVerifiedResolution`'s exact-name pass merges identical names before it looks at hints. Fixed in three layers so no single one has to hold:
- the undecidable mention/row pairs are passed to the resolver as `RESOLUTION_SKIP_PAIRS` (alongside the `DISTINCT_FROM` pairs);
- `LLMVerifiedResolution` keeps any node named in a hint out of its exact-name phase, so it reaches the embedding phase where `_settled` already honours them;
- the merge loop (`_keep_undecidable_mentions_apart`) refuses a mention→row merge when two keyed rows in the cluster could own it, for resolvers that ignore hints.
Default `finalize()` now keeps `alice_smith__person`, `e-1__person`, `e-2__person`, reports both in `probable_duplicates`, and makes no resolution LLM call for them (`test_the_default_finalize_does_not_give_the_mention_to_either_row_either`, `test_a_mention_two_rows_could_own_is_not_the_resolvers_to_hand_out`, `test_declared_distinct_and_settled_pairs_are_not_merged_on_their_name`).

**2. `John Smith` / `John_Smith` → one node (High)** — `_ambiguous_names` compared `display.lower()` while `compute_entity_id` also replaces spaces, so both rows spelled `john_smith__person`. The collision check now derives the same id the rows get; the two rows land as `e1__person` / `e2__person` (`test_names_that_differ_only_where_the_id_flattens_them_stay_apart`).

